{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["D30", "R60"],
      "pins": ["D30.2", "D30.4", "R60.1", "R60.2"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT", "Net-(D30-K2)"],
      "observed": "The second LED section of D30 is connected across the same net: D30 pin 4 (A2) is directly on ESTOP_OUT, while D30 pin 2 (K2) is connected through R60 back to ESTOP_OUT.",
      "expected": "D30 is an LED_Dual_KKAA part, so the K2/A2 pair should be connected between a source/LED-power net and a sink/control net (for example anode to /LED_3.3V_POWER_NONINV and cathode through R60 to ESTOP_OUT). An LED should not have both terminals tied to the same node.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"66\") (name \"ESTOP_OUT\") (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\") ... (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"2\") ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"128\") (name \"Net-(D30-K2)\") (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"K2\") (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"1\") ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(libpart (lib \"cm4-robot\") (part \"LED_Dual_KKAA\") ... (pin (num \"2\") (name \"K2\") ...) (pin (num \"4\") (name \"A2\") ...)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The e-stop/status LED associated with D30's second section cannot light because there is no voltage across it; the board loses the intended visual indication.",
      "confidence": 0.86
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U11.4", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "Both ground pins of the isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 are tied to the same GND net: U10 pin 4 GND1 and U10 pin 5 GND2 are on GND, and U11 pin 4 GND1 and U11 pin 5 GND2 are also on GND.",
      "expected": "If the design intends isolated CAN buses using isolated CAN transceivers, GND1 and GND2 should be separate ground domains to preserve galvanic isolation. The specification explicitly describes two isolated CAN buses driven through isolated CAN transceivers.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"79\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") ...) (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") ...) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") ...) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") ...)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN transceivers' galvanic isolation is defeated; the CAN side is not isolated from the board ground, which can introduce ground loops and removes the intended isolation protection.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The strongest issue is D30's second LED section being tied across the same net, preventing it from functioning as an indicator. A secondary concern is that the isolated CAN transceivers have both ground domains tied to common GND, which contradicts the stated isolated CAN bus architecture."
}

